{
  "statement": "Congress must mandate architectural data silos for AI development. Information security requires boundaries that fail safely, not filters that fail silently. Large language models mathematically cannot unlearn the personal data they ingest. Researchers routinely execute data extraction attacks against production models. They recover exact names, addresses, and phone numbers directly from the training weights. Once developers bake personal information into a unified neural network, post-hoc redaction becomes impossible. Because developers cannot guarantee data deletion, regulators must require physical data separation to prevent systemic privacy breaches.\n\nRegulators must reject the accelerationist premise that unified data harvesting is necessary for AI progress. Fundamental civil liberties must legally supersede corporate convenience. Accelerationists correctly argue that unified datasets allow AI agents to cross-reference your calendar, medical history, and financial records to anticipate your needs. The tech industry disguises this mass surveillance as \"agent utility.\" However, a single conglomerate controlling a unified dataset of citizen behavior creates an extreme information asymmetry. Historical evidence demonstrates that such asymmetry inevitably leads to systemic abuse. Therefore, regulators must dismantle this surveillance architecture by enforcing strict data silos to protect individual autonomy.\n\nEnforcing dataset silos forces pragmatic, privacy-preserving engineering rather than a lazy reliance on mass data hoarding. Effective regulation drives innovation toward compliance. When Congress passed the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act, industry lobbyists predicted the privacy mandates would destroy medical innovation. Instead, developers built robust systems that protect data while delivering critical services. AI developers can similarly adopt federated learning to train models across siloed datasets without exfiltrating personal records. Mandating silos does not kill AI progress. It simply forces companies to build secure infrastructure instead of strip-mining the public square.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "🗄️",
      "tooltip": "A data silo is like a bank vault; it ensures assets remain compartmentalized and secure from systemic breaches."
    },
    {
      "symbol": "🪤",
      "tooltip": "Unified data is like a mousetrap; it offers the bait of convenience to capture your personal autonomy."
    }
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {
      "claim": "Congress must mandate architectural data silos for AI development.",
      "targets": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "Large language models mathematically cannot unlearn the personal data they ingest.",
      "targets": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The tech industry disguises mass surveillance as 'agent utility' to justify unified data harvesting.",
      "targets": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "AI developers can adopt federated learning to train models across siloed datasets without exfiltrating personal records.",
      "targets": []
    }
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {
      "assumption": "Federated learning and differential privacy can achieve commercially viable AI performance without requiring centralized data.",
      "if_wrong": "The tradeoff between AI capability and privacy becomes much starker, requiring society to choose between abandoning advanced AI development or accepting the end of digital privacy."
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Current data extraction attacks against LLMs represent fundamental mathematical flaws rather than temporary engineering bugs.",
      "if_wrong": "Future software patches could make unified datasets perfectly safe, rendering architectural silos an unnecessary regulatory burden."
    }
  ]
}

