ue that mathematical abstractions like differential privacy render physical silos obsolete, and the Skeptic correctly notes that models mathematically cannot unlearn personal data post-ingestion. However, mathematical alignment in an embedding space is opaque to regulators; if a cryptographic abstraction fails, it fails invisibly, whereas silos provide structural boundaries that auditors can actively test and measure. This argument relies on the assumption that physical or architectural boundaries can actually be maintained in highly dynamic, distributed AI training environments without breaking core functionalities. Assuming these boundaries remain technically viable, regulators must mandate architectural silos to force developers to prove their privacy strategies work, ensuring progress remains conditional on verifiable safeguards.",
