- AN-32 (system): Fact-check (supported): The claim accurately describes how hackers exploit artificial intelligence, though the speaker misidentifies a corporate blog post as an academic paper. On February 7, 2026, engineers at the cybersecurity firm InstaTunnel published an article titled 'Differential Privacy Reversal via LLM Feedback.' The authors explain how attackers use public AI models as 'oracles' to uncover hidden personal data. Hackers query the model and analyze its logits—the raw mathematical scores a model assigns to its possible answers. An attacker analyzes these scores to determine if the model memorized a specific person's records during training.

The InstaTunnel article synthesizes peer-reviewed research published between 2024 and 2026. Computer scientists at recent academic conferences demonstrated that large language models remain vulnerable to Membership Inference Attacks. In these attacks, hackers bypass privacy software by studying how confidently a model predicts specific text. Because large language models memorize sensitive data, software that anonymizes records frequently fails to protect users.
